      "note": "Pokorny IEW 849 has 'puk̑-2', 'Meaning: to enclose, put together', with exactly the three members Nourai draws: 'Avestan pusa- f. \"headband\"; gr. ἄμπυξ m. \"headband\", Adverb πύκα \"tight, firm, dense\", ... πυκινός, newer πυκνός \"dense, tight, firm\"; alb. puth \"kusse\", actually \"umarme\"'. Watkins calls it 'puk-1. To make fast. ... Greek pukinos, later puknos ... [Pok. 2. puk- 849]'. Mann, IE Comparative Dictionary, s.v. puk̑-, glosses it 'fit, enclose, hug, embrace, encircle' and lists 'Av. pusā, f. \"diadem\"; Per. pūse, id.; Gk. púka ...; puknós, pukinós ...; cf. ám-pux, gs: -pukos'. So root, page and gloss ('bundled together') are all sound. One nuance worth recording: Walde-Pokorny (1927) II 82-83 had NOT yet joined Av. pusā- to this root — there it appears under the neighbouring root 'puq-, punq- \"dicht behaart, buschig\"' (with Skt. puccha- 'tail', Gmc. *fuhsaz 'fox'), 'zu vermutlich av. pusā f. \"Diadem\" (Zubatý KZ 31, 13, Bthl. Wb. 911 f. zw.; ursprünglich ein pelzbesetzter Turban?)'. Pokorny 1959 moved it; Nourai follows the 1959 arrangement, which is also Klein's and Mann's.",
